  "response": "The controversy over the Channeled Scablands began with a single bold claim. J Harlen Bretz proposed in 1923 that a catastrophic flood had carved the Channeled Scablands, an interpretation he drew from dry falls, giant ripple marks, and anastomosing channels incised into the basalt of the Columbia Plateau. The 1923 proposal rested on an earlier statement of the idea: Bretz first published his flood hypothesis in 1910, and that prior publication confirms how long he had been developing the catastrophic reading of the plateau. Whereas Bretz read the landscape as the product of one overwhelming deluge, his critics argued that the scablands were formed by ordinary gradual river and glacial erosion, the slow and uniform processes that early twentieth-century geology regarded as the proper basis for explanation. That gradualist position was itself a consequence of the strong opposition Bretz faced at a 1927 meeting of the Geological Society of Washington in Washington, D.C., where the confrontation pushed the assembled specialists to commit publicly to slow erosion as their counter-explanation. Even though that 1927 opposition was fierce, Bretz did not abandon the catastrophic interpretation, and the tension between his conviction and the hostility of his audience persisted for decades.\n\nThe weakest point in the hypothesis was hydrological rather than geomorphic. Bretz's early papers did not identify a convincing source for the floodwater, and this omission came before Joseph Thomas Pardee identified Glacial Lake Missoula as the source of the floodwater, a step that supplied the missing reservoir for a flood of the required magnitude. Pardee's lake occupied the valleys of western Montana, and its shorelines remain etched on the hillsides above the modern city of Missoula. Glacial Lake Missoula was impounded by an ice dam where a lobe of the Cordilleran Ice Sheet blocked the Clark Fork River. By contrast, the ice dam that held back Glacial Lake Missoula was formed by the Laurentide Ice Sheet, the vast continental glacier that covered much of interior North America.\n\nThe mechanism of release has been debated with comparable care. At least one of two processes accounts for the failure, perhaps both acting together: the Lake Missoula ice dam failed by floating as the lake deepened, lifting the ice once the water column exceeded roughly nine-tenths of the dam's height, or the Lake Missoula ice dam failed by water tunneling beneath the ice, enlarging subglacial conduits until the barrier collapsed. Each process could have drained the lake rapidly enough to send a wall of water westward across the plateau toward the Columbia River gorge.\n\nA related question concerned the number of events, and here the possibilities are exclusive. Either the Missoula floods occurred as a single flood event, as Bretz originally envisioned, or the Missoula floods occurred as multiple repeated flood events, with the ice dam reforming and failing in cycles; the two accounts cannot both be true, and one of them must hold.\n\nField evidence ultimately settled the broader dispute. Richard Waitt concluded in 1980 that the Touchet Beds record dozens of separate floods, identifying stacked rhythmites in the slackwater sediments of southern Washington, each capped by evidence of subaerial exposure. Waitt's analysis of the Touchet Beds led the 1965 INQUA field conference participants to conclude that Bretz's catastrophic flood interpretation was correct, and that verdict resolved the long tension between the 1927 opposition and Bretz's persistence, since geologists who examined the scablands directly now endorsed the catastrophic origin he had defended. Institutional recognition followed. Bretz received the Penrose Medal in 1979; in other words, the Geological Society of America awarded Bretz its highest honor in 1979. The honor marked the formal reversal of a profession that had once dismissed his work, and it acknowledged that catastrophic processes, properly documented, belong within the explanatory repertoire of geology rather than outside it.",
